From 0039cb7c47d094f61c739e191fd70c1e0adb48a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 02:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Draw a television's picture in perspective, not bent along a
 diagonal

DrawSurfaceQuad handed ImGui a television's four projected corners as one image
quad. ImGui splits it into two triangles and interpolates the texture linearly in
screen space, which a perspective projection does not: the picture was right at
the four corners and bent everywhere between them, kinked along the diagonal the
triangles share, and the bend changed as the player walked round the set. That is
the report -- the picture moves when the screen is looked at from another angle --
and it is invisible to every log: the screen is in the right place, the right
size, drawing every frame. Seen square it is exact, which is how a set is usually
checked. Measured from ImGui's own vertex output against a pinhole ground truth,
it is 102 px on a 1.16 m television 1.5 m away turned 50 degrees, and 307 px on
the 150 ft cinema seen 35 degrees off-axis.

The four corners determine the plane's whole map to the screen (a homography,
closed form). ScreenTessellation.hpp solves it, bounds its second derivatives in
closed form, and picks the smallest grid of cells inside which linear
interpolation errs by less than 0.75 px, capped at 48 x 48; PerspectiveImage.hpp
queues that grid with exact vertices. A set seen square is one cell -- the same
single quad and four vertices as before -- and a quad the map refuses (non-finite,
degenerate, or not convex, which is what a corner behind the eye looks like after
the divide) is drawn exactly as before. Cells wholly outside the view are not
queued. The cells share one descriptor and tint, so ImGui merges them into one
draw command: the most oblique set costs 9216 vertices and still one draw.

The pointer was already placed perspective-exactly (MediaScreens interpolates the
world corners and projects), so on an oblique set the cursor and the element it
appeared to be over disagreed by the same error; drawing the picture correctly
makes them agree.

Tests, both registered beside ScreenMotion's:

  * Open77.ScreenTessellation.Tests -- the arithmetic against an independent
    pinhole model: four corners reproduce the interior, the chosen grid is within
    the bound it reports and the bound within the tolerance below the cap, the
    bound is not vacuous against brute force, the grid is watertight, culling and
    every refusal.
  * Open77.PerspectiveImage.Tests -- the drawing, through a real ImGui draw list:
    a set seen square is the identical single quad; an oblique one puts every
    texel the GPU would sample within the tolerance of where the plane puts it
    (102 px -> 0.43 px), in one draw command, watertight, uvs covering the whole
    picture; a set seen from behind; the cinema partly out of view (307 px ->
    0.51 px, cells out of view skipped); a crossed quad and a NaN corner drawn as
    before.

Not done here, and unchanged by this: depth. The overlay pass still has no depth
buffer, so a screen is still drawn over anything between it and the camera --
hands, bodies, walls. That needs the game's depth at the moment the overlay
draws, which a capture has to locate first.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Qum3jofThN9KuYZN8xcYdD
